* Step 3: When running in the cloud, I think the most common practice would be to run six different one-core instances instead of one instance with six or more cores. The reason would be not just to try to avoid multiple cores competing for the memory bandwidth of a single physical machine, but also the fact that a single N-core spot instance (for N > 2) will usually cost more than N times the cost of a one-core spot instance, per hour of usage, especially for N larger than 2. However, if using a high-performance GPU in the cloud, these are usually attached to a virtual machine with a large number of cores, so this multi-core setup could be used to run multiple tsfactor_cpu processes in parallel with a single tsfactor process, although that is: probably not cost-effective.

I have attached the logs for:

 Step_3_6cores_tsfactor_cpu.log
 Step_3_4cores_tsfactor_cpu.log
 Step_3_2cores_tsfactor_cpu.log
 Step_3_1core_tsfactor_cpu.log

 Step_3_6cores_factor_ec.log
 Step_3_4cores_factor_ec.log
 Step_3_2cores_factor_ec.log
 Step_3_1core_factor_ec.log

These ran on a WSL virtual machine with 16GB of memory.

Memory usage per process for tsfactor_cpu peaked at about 7.0% of 16GB memory; memory usage per process for factor_ec peaked in the mid-to-high 11% range. The peaks occurred during the GCD phase.

With one simultaneous process, factor_ec and tsfactor_cpu have about the same runtime. With more simultaneous processes, factor_ec slows down more than tsfactor_cpu does.

